#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Wave 39: Just Teens, a First Time — the next age band's turn.

Nine waves of Little Hands closed the age-3/age-4 gap at parity
(160 against 160, wave 38). The data audit for this wave
recomputed coverage per age and per primary category, and the
thinnest shelf in the whole archive is no longer at the young
end: ages 13 and 14 carry 76 activities each against 446 at
age 12 — a cliff, not a slope — and inside that band the
primary-category cells are the thinnest in the vault: seasons
1, outdoor 2, pe-movement 3, music-rhythm 5, rainy-day 5,
icebreakers 6, crafts 6, holidays 8, stem 8, quiet-focus 14,
team-community 18. Almost everything up there is spillover
from records written for younger rooms (bands like 5-14 and
8-14); only a handful of records were ever written FOR
thirteen- and fourteen-year-olds.

So this wave mirrors the Little Hands shape at the other end
of childhood: twelve records banded 13-14 ONLY, one for every
primary category plus a second for seasons (the floor, at 1),
written for the room they are actually for — longer sessions,
real responsibility, real tools, real audiences, and no
younger-kid ceremony dressed up as teen content. Result,
asserted in the wave log: total 703 -> 715; age-13 coverage
76 -> 88 and age-14 coverage 76 -> 88, every other age
unchanged; the age-13/14 primary floor rises 1 -> 3; and the
pack is an honest twelve, so the 28th collection — "Just
Teens, a First Time" — unlocks at the validator's own 8-12
pick rule, no padding and no lowered bar.

Deliberate neighbours, kept on purpose and named here:
- adopt-a-tree-year-log vs seasonal-rounds-phenology-wheel
  (a crafted wheel charting the year's events): the wheel
  there is made and finished; the log here is kept monthly
  for a whole year and handed to next year's custodians —
  longitudinal keeping, not a chart.
- seed-library-opening-day vs seed-starting-cups and the
  other sowing records: those plant seeds; this one runs a
  lending library — germination-tested packets, honest
  percentages, a ledger — the institution, not the planting.
- compass-course-three-flags: no compass record exists
  anywhere on the shelf (checked); bearings and pace counts
  are new ground entire.
- referee-run-round-robin vs every game it officiates: the
  games exist; the officiating — whistle, scoresheet,
  dispute protocol, rotation — has never been the record.
- ten-minute-set-handover vs haunted-house-soundtrack
  (composing a score for a house): that is composition for a
  scene; this is live curation for a floor — track choice,
  energy arc, and a handover the dancers never notice.
- indoor-clue-chain-architects vs indoor-scavenger-hunt
  (find it, don't move it, 6-12) and escape-room-build
  (11-14, a room of puzzles and locks): a hunt follows a
  list; an escape room escapes a room; a clue CHAIN is
  sequential logic designed by its architects, calibrated
  by playtest, and run by another team — design is the game.
- hot-seat-kind-questions vs two-minute-mingle (pairs in
  motion): the mingle moves; the seat is still — one person,
  the whole group's kind attention, and an absolute pass.
- repair-cafe-first-session: no repair record exists on the
  shelf (checked); mending, triage, and the honesty of
  "beyond this cafe" are new ground entire.
- midnight-around-the-world vs midnight-countdown-rehearsal
  (rehearsing one countdown, younger) and new-year-time-capsule
  (objects sealed for later): this is a hosted evening that
  follows midnight across the time zones on a map, with the
  arithmetic done live and each city greeted properly.
- wind-turbine-blade-trials vs spiderweb-weight-bridge and
  the other load tests: those test a structure until it
  fails; this isolates one blade variable at a time against
  two honest measures (revolutions, lifted clips).
- commonplace-book-first-pages vs harvest-moon-journal and
  the other journals (recording your own days): a commonplace
  book copies OTHER voices — passages chosen, transcribed by
  hand, and indexed — curation, not diary.
- teach-back-to-the-youngers vs peer-skill-swap (peers
  teaching peers, 12-14): the swap is horizontal; the
  teach-back is vertical — a prepared session delivered to
  younger children, with rehearsal and safeguarding rules.

Two drafted records were cut before the pack ran, on the
standing near-dupe discipline: an anonymous appreciation
post office for the holidays slot — kindness-post-office
already runs a Valentine's post office at 4-9, and older
postmasters do not make it a new record — and an egg-drop
review board for the STEM slot — egg-drop-challenge already
runs the drop; a clipboard jury does not change the physics.
Both slots were rebuilt from genuinely empty ground: no
midnight-across-the-zones record and no turbine record
existed anywhere on the shelf.

The drama/coding lane, recorded not padded (standing rule):
still no drama or coding category among the eleven, and no
validator tag names one. This wave does not invent a twelfth
category either. The lane stays an honest named gap.

Safety notes are practical only — no medical claims anywhere.

Run once: dedupes by id, writes data/activities.json and
data/collections.json. Run scripts/build.py afterwards.
"""
import json, os, re, sys

