      safety="The book's privacy is its safety system and it is absolute: a commonplace book is read by its keeper and by nobody else without the keeper's invitation, given freely for that reading — not borrowed, not passed around in the sharing round, not photographed, not quoted back at its keeper in jest, and the adult keeps their own book under the same law and is seen to keep it. Provenance honesty is the record's other law, taught as craft rather than policed as crime: a passage copied from memory is labelled from memory, a source half-remembered is written as half-remembered, and nothing in a keeper's book may be dressed in a grander source than it has — because the book's whole authority, decades from now, rests on its keeper being able to trust what its younger self wrote. Materials are ordinary and personal — pens chosen by their keepers, books kept by their owners — and the session's only physical rule is the room's ordinary one: a silent working room is arranged so that anyone who needs to move, stretch, or step out can do so without ceremony or explanation.",
